---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="About" description="Why CricBreakdown exists and who writes it.">
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-5 md:px-8 pt-16 pb-24">
    <span class="stat-chip text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-leather">About</span>
    <h1 class="font-display font-bold text-3xl md:text-5xl text-pitch mt-2 mb-8">
      Cricket, taken apart.
    </h1>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:font-display prose-headings:text-pitch prose-a:text-leather">
      <p>
        CricBreakdown started with a simple frustration: most cricket coverage
        tells you the score, then moves on. It rarely stops to explain why a
        match actually swung the way it did, or whether a team's auction
        strategy holds up once you look past the headline signings.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site is written by Ansar, a cricket fan who follows the IPL and
        world cricket closely and wanted a place to dig into the stats and
        stories that don't fit into a scorecard — team-by-team breakdowns,
        bowling and batting analysis, auction economics, and the history that
        gives current form its context.
      </p>
      <h2>What you'll find here</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Stat-led breakdowns of IPL teams, seasons, and auctions</li>
        <li>Bowling and batting analysis that goes beyond highlight reels</li>
        <li>Cricket history and retrospectives</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>What this site isn't</h2>
      <p>
        CricBreakdown is not a live-score or breaking-news site — for that,
        ESPNcricinfo and Cricbuzz do a great job already. This is a place for
        the analysis you read after the scoreline, not instead of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        CricBreakdown is independent fan commentary and analysis. It is not
        affiliated with, endorsed by, or connected to the BCCI, ICC, IPL, or
        any cricket franchise mentioned on this site.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
